From e6b200c3962c5dc1780930ff0bbecb8d98da29d8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hassan Jawaid <hassan@vapt.uk>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 03:24:23 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Validate and bound the URLs the inbox handler fetches

The inbox endpoint fetches the URL given in the "actor" field of the
received activity before any signature has been validated, so any
unauthenticated caller can choose an address the server will request.
The inbox URL used for delivery comes from that same remote document,
which means the server also signs a request to an address of the
sender's choosing with its own private key.

Add f2ap/safefetch.py, which allows only the http and https schemes and
refuses host names that resolve to loopback, private, link-local,
multicast, reserved or unspecified addresses, to carrier-grade NAT
(100.64.0.0/10, which ipaddress does not report as private) and to the
NAT64 prefixes; IPv4-mapped IPv6 addresses are judged on the address
they carry. It applies a 10-second timeout, because the fetch is a
blocking call inside an async handler and an address that never answers
would otherwise hold the event loop.

Redirects are followed, not refused: redirecting actor URLs and
delegated WebFinger are both normal between instances, so refusing them
would break federation with ordinary peers. Each hop is re-validated
before it is requested and the chain is capped at five, so a redirect
cannot be used to reach an address that was never checked.

Route the actor fetch, the webfinger lookup and the delivery through it,
and handle connection and JSON errors so a failed fetch no longer
surfaces as an unhandled 500.

Reported by Hassan Jawaid.
---
